<div class="qul-docs">
  <p>
    In Arabic, a <strong>root</strong> is the small set of consonants — usually three — that a whole
      family of related words is built from. Grouping words by root reveals how Quranic vocabulary connects:
      <span class="qpc-hafs">الرَّحْمَـٰن</span>, <span class="qpc-hafs">الرَّحِيم</span> and
      <span class="qpc-hafs">رَحْمَة</span> all come from <span class="qpc-hafs">ر ح م</span>.
  </p>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
    This resource gives the roots of an <strong>ayah at a time</strong>: for each ayah, the roots of its
      words in the order the words appear. If you need the root of one specific word instead, use the
      <a href="/resources/morphology">Word root</a> resource.
  </div>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Ayah <code>73:4</code> has these roots:</p>
  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>73:4</span></div>
    <pre><code class="qpc-hafs">زيد رتل قرا رتل</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Export format</h2>
  <p>
    QUL exports this data as a SQLite database with one row per ayah.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Column</th>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>What it holds</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ayah_key</code></td>
        <td>TEXT</td>
        <td>The ayah, in <code>surah:ayah</code> form — for example <code>73:4</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>text</code></td>
        <td>TEXT</td>
        <td>The root of each word in the ayah, in word order, separated by spaces.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-warning">
    The roots are listed in word order but are not individually numbered. To line them up with the words
    of the ayah you also need the word by word Quran script, available on the
    <a href="/resources/quran-script">Quran Scripts</a> page.
  </div>
</div>
